"Acceptance Is Not Speed: Length Inflation in Training-Free Lossy Speculative Decoding (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23818v1)\nciteturn41242view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.23818v1\",\"lineno\":200}); Total lines: 755\nL161: GSM8K, AIME24, and LongBench-v2 are graded by exact match on the final answer; HumanEval and LiveCodeBench by executing the generated code against the problem’s tests. A run that never produces an answer is scored wrong. MT-Bench is scored by an LLM judge (Table cite94†B4 ). Case counts, generation budgets, and lossless references are listed in Tables cite95†B1 and cite96†D3 . A run that ends at its budget is counted as cut off.\nL162: #### Metrics.\nL163: We report five quantities: $\\bar{\\ell}$, the mean accepted length (draft tokens accepted per round); $\\bar{L}$, the mean completion length in tokens; $R$, the mean number of verifier rounds; the mean per-request wall-clock time, measured at the client at batch size 1 and excluding server start; and accuracy, the pass rate over the dataset’s cases (for MT-Bench, the mean judge score). Each case is run once per arm.\nL164: Every ratio to lossless is computed on the same cases for both arms, and its uncertainty is a 95% paired bootstrap interval over cases (10 000 resamples). A cell on a graded dataset (all but MT-Bench) loses accuracy when it falls two or more points below its lossless reference.\nL165: ### 3.2 Matching on mean accepted length\nL166: Because $\\alpha$ is not comparable across methods (Section cite9†2.2 ), we compare methods at matched $\\bar{\\ell}$. A short pilot runs each method’s four $\\alpha$ values (Table cite79†A1 ) on three cases per dataset; we then fix three target values of $\\bar{\\ell}$ and run the full dataset at the $\\alpha$ closest to each. The five methods never share a reachable $\\bar{\\ell}$, so the match is approximate. The four pairs with a standalone drafter and the P-EAGLE pair run each rule at its loosest $\\alpha$ only.\nL167: The tables report each method at its most relaxed setting; Sections cite21†3.3 and cite24†3.6 check that the conclusions do not depend on this choice.\nL168: ### 3.3 Completion length\nL169: \nL170: Relaxation lengthens completions in 248 of the 360 cells (Tables cite97†1 and cite80†3 ): $\\lambda$ has median $1.08$ and mean $1.54$, with the 95% interval above $1$ in 172 cells and below $1$ in 67. Its median varies more with the rule ($0.97$ for spec_casc_tok to $1.39$ for cactus) and the drafter ($0.94$ for standalone drafters to $1.23$ for EAGLE heads) than with the task ($1.04$ on GSM8K to $1.14$ on HumanEval), and cut-off runs make it a lower bound (Section cite25†3.7 ).\nL171: The inflation persists at each rule’s gentlest setting (on the seven pairs run with the matched protocol, median $\\lambda$ $1.07$ against $1.22$ at the loosest, and more rounds than lossless in 97 cells against 94; Table cite98†D4 ) and across seeds (Appendix cite43†C ). At Qwen3-8B’s recommended sampler (temperature $0.6$), mentored_dec, cactus, and spec_casc_tok stop inflating on GSM8K and LiveCodeBench, but spec_casc_opt still inflates by $1.5\\times$ (Appendix cite43†C ).\nL172: Dataset  | mentored  | cactus  | casc-opt  | r-fuzzy  | casc-tok  | mentored  | cactus  | casc-opt  | r-fuzzy  | casc-tok\nL173: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL174:  | GPT-OSS-20B + EAGLE-3 (nebius)  | Qwen3-8B + EAGLE-3 (RedHatAI)\nL175: --- | --- | ---\nL176: GSM8K  | 1.16 (.93)  | 1.61 (.91)  | 1.46 (.94)  | 1.70 (.87)  | 0.96^{∘} (.97)  | 1.03^{∘} (.77)  | 1.04^{∘} (.77)  | 1.33 (.49)  | 0.95^{∘} (.55)  | 0.99^{∘} (.79)\nL177: AIME24  | 1.69 (.63)  | 1.78 (.60)  | 2.48 (.37)  | 2.13 (.50)  | 1.48 (.70)  | 1.01^{∘} (.73)  | 1.26 (.23)  | 1.42 (.30)  | 1.29 (.40)  | 0.98^{∘} (.70)\nL178: HumanEval  | 1.30 (.96)  | 1.79 (.87)  | 1.73 (.82)  | 1.64 (.63)  | 1.05^{∘} (.94)  | 1.08 (.85)  | 1.14 (.75)  | 1.72 (.49)  | 1.65 (.16)  | 1.08 (.85)\nL179: LiveCodeBench  | 1.25 (.84)  | 1.54 (.61)  | 1.64 (.49)  | 1.65 (.34)  | 1.16 (.88)  | 1.05 (.63)  | 1.15 (.44)  | 1.27 (.33)  | 1.07^{∘} (.02)  | 1.00^{∘} (.71)\nL180: MT-Bench  | 1.10^{∘} (6.41)  | 1.18 (4.51)  | 1.09^{∘} (5.74)  | 1.20 (4.58)  | 0.96^{∘} (7.49)  | 1.04^{∘} (6.40)  | 1.16 (2.99)  | 1.22 (3.95)  | 1.12 (3.05)  | 1.04^{∘} (7.21)\nL181: LongBench-v2  | 1.59 (.51)  | 1.77 (.36)  | 1.79 (.47)  | 1.69 (.40)  | 1.13^{∘} (.53)  | 1.06^{∘} (.51)  | 2.10 (.29)  | 1.16 (.37)  | 1.08^{∘} (.44)  | 1.00^{∘} (.49)\nL182: Table 1: Length inflation on the primary pairs (Section cite15†3.1 ) at each method’s most relaxed tested setting: completion length relative to lossless ($\\lambda$; $>1$ is inflation), with the accuracy at that setting in parentheses (MT-Bench: judge score out of 10). ^{∘}: the 95% interval for $\\lambda$ includes $1$ (Table cite99†B5 ). All twelve pairs: Table cite100†D1 .\nL183: ### 3.4 Verifier rounds and time\nL184: The extra acceptance buys few rounds (Tables cite101†2 and cite80†3 , Figures cite102†1 and cite103†2 ): over the 360 cells $R/R^{*}$ has median $0.93$ and mean $1.10$, and 114 cells need more rounds than lossless (67 significantly).\nL185: The round ratio follows length, not acceptance (Spearman $\\rho$ $0.78$ with $\\lambda$, $-0.12$ with the acceptance gain), and the length-corrected model of Equation (cite104†3 ), which at fixed $N_{\\mathrm{draft}}$ reduces to Equation (cite105†5 ), predicts it from the measured length within $2\\%$ at the median.\nL186: Time is worse than rounds on the primary pairs (median ratio $1.01$ against $0.96$; eleven cells save rounds but not time) because on their server a round’s cost grows with the tokens it commits; on a second server they agree (Appendix cite43†C ), so rounds are the hardware-independent measure.\nL187: Dataset  | ment.  | cactus  | c-opt  | r-fuzzy  | c-tok  | ment.  | cactus  | c-opt  | r-fuzzy  | c-tok\nL188:  | GPT-OSS-20B + EAGLE-3 (nebius)  | Qwen3-8B + EAGLE-3 (RedHatAI)\nL189: GSM8K  | 0.83  | 0.99  | 1.07  | 1.14  | 0.83  | 0.93  | 0.90  | 1.05  | 0.76  | 0.95\nL190: AIME24  | 1.18  | 1.07  | 1.70  | 1.37  | 1.22  | 0.94  | 0.55  | 1.06  | 1.16  | 0.93\nL191: HumanEval  | 0.97  | 1.12  | 1.29  | 1.16  | 0.93  | 0.99  | 0.96  | 1.36  | 1.33  | 1.04\nL192: LiveCodeBench  | 0.89  | 0.94  | 1.16  | 1.11  | 0.98  | 0.93  | 0.72  | 1.03  | 0.87  | 0.94\nL193: MT-Bench  | 0.75  | 0.62  | 0.76  | 0.76  | 0.88  | 0.89  | 0.64  | 0.94  | 0.85  | 0.96\nL194: LongBench-v2  | 1.11  | 0.87  | 1.31  | 1.10  | 0.97  | 0.98  | 0.80  | 0.83  | 0.96  | 0.96\nL195: Table 2: Verifier rounds relative to lossless ($R/R^{*}$) on the primary pairs at each method’s most relaxed tested setting (ment., c-: mentored, casc-). Bold: the 95% interval for $R/R^{*}$ lies entirely above $1$ (more rounds than lossless); underline: entirely below $1$. All twelve pairs: Table cite106†D2 . Figure 1: Every tested setting of the primary pairs (151 runs; filled markers GPT-OSS-20B, hollow Qwen3-8B): length inflation against acceptance gain.\nL196: A relaxed rule saves verifier rounds only below the dashed line $\\lambda=(\\bar{\\ell}+1)/(\\bar{\\ell}^{*}+1)$ (Equation (cite105†5 )). All twelve pairs, one panel per rule: Figure cite103†2 . Figure 2: All 360 cells at each rule’s most relaxed tested setting, one panel per rule (log scales; outlined: the primary pairs). A cell saves verifier rounds only below the dashed line $\\lambda=(\\bar{\\ell}+1)/(\\bar{\\ell}^{*}+1)$ (Equation (cite105†5 )).\nL197: Above it: 21, 31, 31, 38, and 11 of each rule’s 72 cells (mean gain 1.21, 1.74, 1.41, 1.22, 1.11), and 111 of 210 cells with an EAGLE-3, EAGLE-1, or P-EAGLE head, 3 of 30 with DSpark, and 18 of 120 with a standalone drafter.\nL198: ### 3.5 Accuracy retention\nL199: \nL200: Relaxation costs accuracy in 198 of the 300 graded cells (lossless references: Tables cite95†B1 and cite96†D3 ; MT-Bench: Appendix cite39†B ). The three aggressive rules lose in 143 of their 180 graded cells, by $0.19$ on average for cactus, $0.14$ for spec_casc_opt, and $0.21$ for r_fuzzy; mentored_dec loses in 33 of 60 cells (mean $0.03$) and spec_casc_tok in 22 of 60 (mean $0.005$).\nL201: The losses follow the inflation: 132 of the 144 graded cells whose $\\lambda$ interval lies above $1$ lose accuracy (mean loss $0.21$), against 66 of the other 156 (mean $0.03$). By task they are largest on code generation, with median losses of $0.09$ on HumanEval and $0.11$ on LiveCodeBench against $0.03$ to $0.04$ on the other graded datasets.\nL202: ### 3.6 Analysis\nL203: Settings that need fewer verifier rounds lose accuracy.  With an EAGLE head, the three aggressive rules need fewer rounds than lossless in 40 of their 105 graded cells, and 35 of these lose accuracy, most where the cut is deepest: cactus cuts rounds to $0.44\\times$ on R1-Distill-Llama-8B/LiveCodeBench and to $0.55\\times$ on Qwen3-8B/AIME24, while accuracy falls from $0.58$ to $0.02$ and from $0.70$ to $0.23$.\nL204: Of the 86 graded cells that save rounds and keep accuracy, 68 use spec_casc_tok, DSpark, or a standalone Qwen3 drafter, the choices closest to the target (below). On the primary pairs, tuning for accuracy saves a few percent of the rounds at best: only 29 of the 50 graded cells have a tested setting within two points of lossless, and the best of these have a median round ratio of $0.96$ (Table cite107†B3 ).\nL205: Keeping accuracy means staying close to the target.  spec_casc_tok, the only rule that can reject what lossless verification accepts (Section cite13†2.3 ), is also the only one that keeps accuracy on average: over its 60 graded cells it loses $0.005$, against $0.03$ to $0.21$ for the other rules, and its $\\lambda$ interval lies above $1$ in 5 of its 72 cells, against 28 to 51.\nL206: Its acceptance gain is the smallest ($1.11\\times$), yet because its completions do not grow (mean $\\lambda$ $0.96$) it needs $0.86\\times$ the rounds of lossless on average.\nL207:  |  |  | mean $\\lambda$  | mean $R/R^{*}$  |\nL208: Target, drafter  | $\\bar{\\ell}^{*}$  | gain  | ment.  | cactus  | c-opt  | r-fuzzy  | c-tok  | all  | ment.  | cactus  | c-opt  | r-fuzzy  | c-tok  | all  | acc. loss\nL209: GPT-OSS-20B\nL210: ---\nL211: EAGLE-3 (nebius)  | 2.28  | 1.38  | 1.35  | 1.61  | 1.70  | 1.67  | 1.12  | 1.49  | 0.96  | 0.93  | 1.22  | 1.10  | 0.97  | 1.04  | 22/25\nL212: EAGLE-3 (RedHatAI)  | 1.49  | 1.35  | 1.31  | 2.32  | 1.36  | 1.84  | 1.07  | 1.58  | 1.14  | 1.01  | 1.16  | 1.52  | 1.05  | 1.18  | 17/25\nL213: Qwen3-8B\nL214: ---\nL215: EAGLE-3 (RedHatAI)  | 1.20  | 1.27  | 1.05  | 1.31  | 1.35  | 1.20  | 1.01  | 1.18  | 0.94  | 0.76  | 1.04  | 0.99  | 0.96  | 0.94  | 18/25\nL216: P-EAGLE (RedHatAI)  | 1.88  | 1.12  | 1.03  | 1.10  | 1.07  | 1.35  | 1.00  | 1.11  | 0.95  | 0.93  | 0.97  | 1.15  | 0.96  | 0.99  | 24/25\nL217: DSpark (DeepSeek)  | 2.35  | 1.23  | 1.03  | 1.03  | 1.23  | 1.11  | 1.00  | 1.08  | 0.85  | 0.83  | 0.87  | 0.90  | 0.87  | 0.86  | 18/25\nL218: Qwen3-0.6B  | 2.92  | 1.24  | 0.92  | 0.90  | 0.92  | 0.97  | 0.94  | 0.93  | 0.75  | 0.67  | 0.78  | 0.67  | 0.87  | 0.75  | 6/25\nL219: Qwen3-1.7B  | 3.60  | 1.25  | 0.89  | 0.89  | 0.91  | 0.89  | 0.91  | 0.90  | 0.69  | 0.69  | 0.69  | 0.69  | 0.78  | 0.71  | 4/25\nL220: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\nL221: ---\nL222: EAGLE-3 (yuhuili)  | 1.67  | 1.57  | 1.94  | 8.85  | 2.70  | 2.15  | 0.84  | 3.30  | 1.51  | 3.96  | 1.01  | 3.00  | 0.70  | 2.03  | 18/25\nL223: EAGLE-1 (yuhuili)  | 1.29  | 1.42  | 1.95  | 6.23  | 3.35  | 2.58  | 0.78  | 2.98  | 1.60  | 3.16  | 1.57  | 2.60  | 0.70  | 1.93  | 19/25\nL224: Llama-3.2-1B  | 3.57  | 1.32  | 0.94  | 1.59  | 0.83  | 1.30  | 0.85  | 1.10  | 0.70  | 1.08  | 0.66  | 0.90  | 0.74  | 0.82  | 16/25\nL225: R1-Distill-Llama-8B\nL226: ---\nL227: EAGLE-3 (yuhuili)  | 1.42  | 1.41  | 1.04  | 2.11  | 1.19  | 1.19  | 0.98  | 1.30  | 0.92  | 0.80  | 0.84  | 1.05  | 0.93  | 0.91  | 16/25\nL228: Llama-3.2-1B  | 1.84  | 1.48  | 1.07  | 2.14  | 1.99  | 1.24  | 1.04  | 1.50  | 0.80  | 0.95  | 1.34  | 1.10  | 0.83  | 1.01  | 20/25\nL229: All twelve pairs  | —  | 1.34  | 1.21  | 2.51  | 1.55  | 1.46  | 0.96  | 1.54  | 0.98  | 1.31  | 1.01  | 1.31  | 0.86  | 1.10  | 198/300\nL230: Table 3: Twelve target-drafter pairs at each rule’s most relaxed tested setting: for each rule, the mean of $\\lambda$ and of $R/R^{*}$ over the pair’s datasets; all: over all the pair’s cells. Bold (underline): the 95% interval lies above (below) $1$ on every dataset. $\\bar{\\ell}^{*}$: mean lossless accepted length; gain: mean $(\\bar{\\ell}+1)/(\\bar{\\ell}^{*}+1)$; acc. loss: graded cells two or more points below lossless. Every cell: Tables cite100†D1 and cite106†D2 . ment., c-: mentored, casc-.\nL231: The inflation is a property of the drafter, not of the target.  The same target inflates very differently with each drafter (Table cite80†3 , Figure cite103†2 ): Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct by $3.30\\times$ on average with its EAGLE-3 head and $1.10\\times$ with Llama-3.2-1B, Qwen3-8B by $1.18\\times$ with its EAGLE-3 head and $0.90\\times$ with Qwen3-1.7B.\nL232: Every EAGLE head inflates ($\\lambda$ interval above $1$ in 132 of 210 cells, below $1$ in 8), and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct with its EAGLE heads is the worst case: its lossless answers run 280 to 4 840 tokens, and cactus multiplies them by $4.0$ to $13.2$.\nL233: Drafters that sample close to the target inflate little: DSpark has median $\\lambda$ $1.06$ and a round interval below $1$ in 22 of its 30 cells, and the standalone Qwen3 drafters lengthen completions significantly in none of their 60 cells and cut rounds by $10\\%$ to $46\\%$, losing accuracy in 10 of 50 graded cells against 18 and 24 of 25 for DSpark and P-EAGLE. A standalone drafter from another model’s distribution inflates again: Llama-3.2-1B on R1-Distill-Llama-8B, up to $3.28\\times$ on LongBench-v2.\nL234: ### 3.7 Why completions get longer\nL235: \nL236: For all twelve pairs the run logs record whether a run hit its budget; for the primary pairs they also record the split between thinking trace and answer and, for 15 traced GPT-OSS-20B cases per dataset and arm, every drafted token’s target probability and both rules’ verdicts. Four measurements follow, at the loosest settings.\nL237: The extra text is thinking, not answer.  On the primary pairs, GPT-OSS-20B’s analysis channel receives at least $92\\%$ of the extra characters in 25 of its 30 cells. Qwen3-8B puts more than $90\\%$ of the extra characters inside its <think> blocks at the median, with one exception: cactus on AIME24 inflates the answer ($9.4\\times$) because 11 of its 30 outputs reopen a closed think block. Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct has no thinking trace, so its inflation, the largest measured, lengthens the answer itself.\nL238: A broad shift with a heavy tail.  On the primary pairs the per-case ratio $L_{\\mathrm{relax}}/L_{\\mathrm{strict}}$ has a median above $1$ in 47 of the 60 cells and a lower quartile below $1$ in 34. Its 90th percentile has median $2.2$ over the cells, and the tenth of cases with the largest increase holds $59\\%$ of the net extra tokens at the median. This is not exact looping: on GPT-OSS-20B at most $2.9\\%$ of a cell’s tokens lie inside a repeated 50-gram (lossless: at most $1.9\\%$).\nL239: Qwen3-8B does loop under spec_casc_opt: $15\\%$ of its AIME24 tokens and $13\\%$ of its LongBench-v2 tokens (lossless $0.4\\%$ and $1.3\\%$), with a mean longest repeat of 2 728 tokens on AIME24.\nL240: Cut-offs understate inflation.  Relaxation raises the share of runs that end at the generation budget in 208 of the 360 cells (median $11\\%$ against $4\\%$ under lossless, where Qwen3-8B already reaches $25\\%$ on GSM8K; per-cell shares in the released tables). On the primary pairs, keeping only the cases that finished under both rules moves $\\lambda$ by at most $0.1$ in 45 of the 60 cells, so the inflation is not an artifact of the cap.\nL241: Relaxation admits tokens the target rates as unlikely.  We split the committed draft tokens of the traced runs into those both rules accept and those only the relaxed rule accepts (Table cite108†B2 ). The relaxed-only tokens are $7\\%$ to $24\\%$ of the committed draft tokens. For the four rules other than spec_casc_tok they have a median target probability of $0.01$ to $0.09$ (shared tokens: $0.78$ to $0.93$) and a mean target rank of 37 to 234.\nL242: spec_casc_tok admits the fewest extra tokens ($7\\%$), and they are the target’s first or second choice (mean rank $0.9$, median probability $0.29$). Lossless verification accepts any token with $q(x)\\leq p(x)$, so a relaxed rule can only add tokens with $q(x)>p(x)$: the overshoot tokens that cite69†Wang et al. (2026a) tie to quality loss. In our traces they also carry the extra length.\nL243: A lossless control agrees: raising the sampling temperature from $1.0$ to $1.2$, which also commits lower-probability tokens, lengthens GPT-OSS-20B completions by $1.27\\times$ on GSM8K and $1.07\\times$ on LiveCodeBench, and $T=1.5$ inflates GSM8K by $3.9\\times$ with accuracy $0.31$ (Appendix cite43†C ). The logs show two things: relaxation commits tokens the target would rarely sample, and the thinking trace then gets longer.\nL244: They do not show why an unlikely token makes a reasoning model think more rather than less.\nL245: ## 4 What the Extra Length Buys\nL246: \nL247: Reasoning models often answer better when they think longer (cite109†Muennighoff et al., 2025 ), so the length that relaxation adds could be useful thinking. Per-token guards that force lossless verification do not shorten completions beyond their tuning cases (Appendix cite52†F ), so we act on whole runs.\nL248: Closing the reasoning trace recovers answers.  We rerun every relaxed run that passes a token threshold near the generation budget without closing its reasoning trace, and at the threshold force the token that closes the trace, the budget forcing of cite109†Muennighoff et al. (2025) ; runs that close their trace earlier are unchanged (Appendix cite52†F ).\nL249: Over 1 132 such runs (five rules, both primary pairs, five graded datasets; Table cite110†4 ), correct answers rise from 126 to 439: 335 runs that failed now answer correctly and 22 that succeeded now fail, 302 and 7 of them on Qwen3-8B.\nL250:  | Qwen3-8B  | GPT-OSS-20B\nL251: Dataset  | forced  | correct  | $+$  | $-$  | forced  | correct  | $+$  | $-$\nL252: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL253: GSM8K  | 307  | 56 $\\to$  | 225  | 171  | 2  | 32  | 4 $\\to$  | 10  | 6  | 0\nL254: AIME24  | 60  | 2 $\\to$  | 6  | 4  | 0  | 36  | 3 $\\to$  | 7  | 7  | 3\nL255: HumanEval  | 205  | 16 $\\to$  | 52  | 37  | 1  | 9  | 2 $\\to$  | 0  | 0  | 2\nL256: LiveCodeBench  | 226  | 11 $\\to$  | 81  | 72  | 2  | 62  | 7 $\\to$  | 16  | 13  | 4\nL257: LongBench-v2  | 157  | 17 $\\to$  | 33  | 18  | 2  | 38  | 8 $\\to$  | 9  | 7  | 6\nL258: Total  | 955  | 102 $\\to$  | 397  | 302  | 7  | 177  | 24 $\\to$  | 42  | 33  | 15\nL259: Table 4: Forcing relaxed runs to close their reasoning trace at a threshold near the generation budget, summed over the five rules at their loosest tested settings. forced: runs still reasoning at the threshold; correct: how many of them are correct without $\\to$ with forcing; $+$: forced runs that go from wrong to correct; $-$: from correct to wrong. Per rule and thresholds: Table cite111†F1 .\nL260: The extra tokens follow the answer on grade-school math and precede it on competition math.  For cases both arms answer correctly, we split the extra tokens at the first point where the output writes the reference answer (Table cite112†F2 ). On GSM8K, $50\\%$ (GPT-OSS-20B) and $82\\%$ (Qwen3-8B) of the extra tokens come after it, and most runs cut off at the budget already contain the answer.\nL261: On AIME24, relaxed runs reach the answer later (median token 3 865 against 1 721 for GPT-OSS-20B), at least $59\\%$ of the extra tokens precede it, and few cut-off runs contain it.\nL262: The extra length is waste.  On problems within the model’s reach, relaxation makes the model continue past an answer it already holds, as lossless reasoning models already do (cite113†Chen et al., 2025 ); on hard problems, it lengthens the route to the answer. In neither case do the extra tokens buy accuracy.\nL263: ## 5 Conclusion\nL264: We evaluated five training-free relaxed verification rules on six benchmarks and twelve target-drafter pairs, measuring completion length, verifier rounds, time, and accuracy rather than acceptance alone. Relaxation does raise the number of draft tokens accepted per round, but the model spends that gain on longer outputs: across the 360 cells the mean length inflation ($1.54\\times$) exceeds the mean acceptance gain ($1.34\\times$).\nL265: Where a rule does decode faster, accuracy drops; the one rule that keeps accuracy close to lossless, spec_casc_tok, is also the one that relaxes least.\nL266: This paper identifies length as what decides the speed of relaxed verification: once our measured length is supplied, the length-corrected model of cite67†Xia et al. (2026) predicts our verifier rounds within about $2\\%$, and the clock departs from it only through the cost of a round, which the model does not contain. Two conclusions follow.\nL267: Acceptance gain is not a measure of speed for a relaxed rule, because relaxation changes what the model generates and not only how fast it is verified; completion length and verifier rounds have to be reported next to it. And the promising direction is not accepting more of the drafter’s tokens but choosing which ones to admit, since the only rule that worked trusted the target more.\nL268: Section 4 also yields a simple mitigation: closing the reasoning trace before the generation budget runs out recovers the answers that inflation cuts off, raising correct answers from 126 to 439 over the 1,132 runs it touches, under all five rules. It does not recover the speed, since these runs still spend most of their budget; closing the trace earlier, where the answer is already written, is the natural next step.\nL269: ## Limitations\nL270: \nL271: #### Matching and settings.\nL272: \nL273: No $\\bar{\\ell}$ is reachable by every method, so the matching of Section cite20†3.2 is best-effort and the tables compare methods at different $\\bar{\\ell}$, each at its most relaxed setting. Each method is tested at four published or reference $\\alpha$ values; other values could change its profile, but the conclusions also hold at the gentlest and the best accuracy-preserving settings (Section cite21†3.3 ; Tables cite98†D4 and cite107†B3 ).\nL274: #### Budget censoring.\nL275: \n"
